faq:
  - q: "What should you do after getting rejected from a job interview?"
    a: "Five steps: (1) Send a gracious reply to the recruiter — thank them for the process and ask if there is feedback they can share. (2) Run an honest self-assessment of where the interview was weakest. (3) Diagnose the type of rejection: positioning problem, performance problem, or context problem (internal candidate, budget change). (4) Feed your diagnosis back into your preparation before the next opportunity. (5) Keep the relationship open — companies frequently hire from their silver-medal pools when the first-choice candidate falls through."
  - q: "Should you ask for feedback after a rejection?"
    a: "Always try — framed professionally. 'I appreciate the process and understand the decision. If there is any feedback you can share on where I might strengthen my candidacy, I would genuinely value it.' Most recruiters will not provide detailed feedback for legal and policy reasons, but some will. Asking signals maturity and professionalism. The worst outcome is they decline — and even asking keeps the relationship positive."
  - q: "What does getting rejected after a final interview mean?"
    a: "Final-round rejection usually means one of three things: (1) An internal or referral candidate edged you out — not a reflection of your capability. (2) You were a strong candidate but not the clear top choice — a calibration issue with the role or level, not a disqualification. (3) Something specific in the final conversation weakened your case — usually an answer that landed at the wrong level, a moment of poor communication, or a mismatch on compensation or timeline. Diagnosing which matters for learning from it."
  - q: "How do I stay motivated after multiple rejections?"
    a: "Reframe each rejection as data, not verdict. A rejection tells you something specific: about your positioning, your performance, or the competitive landscape. Candidates who treat rejections as data iterate faster and land stronger offers. The most effective job seekers run systematic retrospectives, adjust, and keep moving. The candidates who spiral treat each rejection as evidence that something is wrong with them — and that belief makes the next interview worse."
  - q: "Can you reapply to a company after being rejected?"
    a: "Yes — after 6–12 months, especially if you have meaningfully strengthened your candidacy in the interim. Email the recruiter, reference the previous process positively, and note specifically what has changed: a new role, a new project, a new skill, a promotion. Companies hire from rejection pools regularly. The key is a genuine reason to reconsider — not just persistence."
